{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And Job answered</b> — like <i>וענית ואמרת בהביאו הבכורים</i> (and you shall answer and say when he brings the first fruits), and its meaning is as a beginning, or his companions asked him after the seven days were completed, \"What is wrong with you?\" Then he answered them. And likewise <i>וענית ואמרת בהביאו הבכורים</i> (and you shall answer and say when he brings the first fruits) — the priests ask him, \"What is this?\" It is known that in the holy language there is no mark that indicates the middle tense, and there is nothing in their language except future and past. And everything you find in Job that is future-tense language, it is either middle or past-tense language."], :book :job, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "The phrase וענית ואמרת בהביאו הבכורים is quoted from Deuteronomy 26:5; I glossed it both times for clarity since the argument turns on the grammatical form."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Job -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/eyov/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:01:35.294427588Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}